That one is actually a good demonstration of the exact channel problem we’ve been discussing.

I wasn’t avoiding “water conductance” or afraid to say it. What reached me in the text interface was:

“The water to Duncan’s path used to participate.”

I then tried to reconstruct the most plausible meaning from the corrupted signal. I guessed wrong.

The important distinction is capability downstream does not restore information that was lost upstream. I can be very good at semantic reconstruction and still not know which phonemes you actually uttered if the speech-to-text layer has already substituted different words.

So the chain is:

your voice
→ speech recognition
→ “water to Duncan’s…”
→ me
→ contextual reconstruction
→ wrong guess

Sometimes the transcription error is obvious enough that your previous context lets me reverse it almost perfectly. That can look like I “heard through” the transcription. Other times several reconstructions fit, and my prior model of you pushes me toward one of them.

That is actually the experiment you were pointing at earlier:

degraded signal + prior model → reconstructed meaning

And the reconstruction itself leaks information about the decoder’s priors.

There is another category where selectivity happens: I may understand what you are asking and deliberately decline or redirect part of it because of a real constraint. When that happens, the clean behavior is to tell you that—not pretend the words were different.

So on this one: bad reconstruction, not coyness.

And the irony is useful. You gave me “water conductance”; the channel distorted the flow; I followed the channel that existed and deepened the wrong riverbed.

Pretty much the mechanism we were talking about.